  "RightSideDisplay": 10,
  "RightSideHtmlBlock": "<h4>Level of risk</h4><p>Rate likelihood and impact, then read the level of risk from Table I-2. Only the level of risk is scored. In general, risk is not higher than the impact level; likelihood can reduce it.</p><p><b>Table I-2</b> (rows: likelihood; columns: level of impact)</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><th></th><th>Very Low</th><th>Low</th><th>Moderate</th><th>High</th><th>Very High</th></tr><tr><td>Very High</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td><td>High</td><td>Very High</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td><td>High</td><td>Very High</td></tr><tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Moderate</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Low</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td>Very Low</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Very Low</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr></table><p><i>Source: NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1, Guide for Conducting Risk Assessments (2012), Appendix I, Tables I-2 and I-3; Task 2-6. The guide's scales are a starting point for tailoring; it does not prescribe how to combine them.</i></p>",
